pancake treats performance as a public contract: `zig build check` pins exact
costs on the wire path, and the charts below report everything else as a dated
snapshot from `zig build bench-site-report`. Measure your own machine. No
"N× faster" folklore elsewhere on the site. Ladder ceilings:
[ladder](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/ladder.md).

## What the build check covers

Assertions that fail `zig build check` if they drift. These are not wall-clock
claims:

- **One alloc per steady inbound delivery** on the borrowed-frame path (`== 1`).
- **Outbound encode budgets**, resolve without a resolution copy, model struct
 ceilings, flat GC export churn, and zero vat-alloc on a steady hosted turn.
- **~2 KiB warmed core session state**: a design budget for high fan-out, not
 live socket RSS. Exact ceilings:
 [ladder](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/ladder.md).

## What the benchmarks report

These print numbers; they do not check them. The one wall-clock assertion is
`zig build perf-check` (ADR-0073, part of `check`): a coarse ReleaseFast tripwire on
the ladder's ceilings, held loose enough that machine noise cannot trip it. Every chart
and table below is regenerated from live runs by `zig build bench-site-report`, a dated
snapshot, not a standing claim.

```=html
<!-- bench:stamp GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<p class="bench-stamp"><em>Measured 2026-07-17 on one machine: Linux 6.17.0-1021-nvidia aarch64, 20 logical CPUs, 122 GiB RAM, Racket v9.2 CS, Spritely Goblins v0.18.0, with the <code>pancake</code> archives built by Zig 0.16.0 in ReleaseFast. Measure your own with the commands below.</em></p>
```

Cautions: charts are **ReleaseFast** (Debug is much slower; check `mode=`).
Footprint is **sans-io core heap**, not live socket RSS. A 500k socket load is
not a checked claim here.

## Codec throughput

Syrup packs a message into compact, variable-length byte records.
Cap'n Proto lays the same message out as larger, fixed-width words with every field at a
known offset. `bench-codec` times encode and decode of one representative call on each
codec: a method invocation with a small mixed-type argument list. Timing the same message
on both makes the per-message nanoseconds comparable even though the MB/s figures move
with frame size. This is a small message on purpose. Cap'n Proto's edge is constant-time
access over large or field-heavy payloads, a shape this does not measure. The trade is the
wire size you see here. The [codecs]($link.page('codecs')) page weighs the full picture.
Measure your own: `zig build bench-codec -Doptimize=ReleaseFast`.

```=html
<!-- bench:codec GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
