## Frame ownership and dispatch

One owned-frame alloc per steady delivery (`== 1`); Frame↔FrameView projection
correctness; model struct sizes. Ideas: [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')).
Measure: `zig build bench-frame-view -Doptimize=ReleaseFast`.

```=html
<!-- bench:frame-view GENERATED by `zig build bench-site-report`; edits are overwritten -->
<table>
<tr><th>metric</th><th>value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Allocations per delivered turn</td><td>1 (gated <code>== 1</code>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Frame-view projections exercised</td><td>1,000,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Argument / Message struct bytes</td><td>88 / 224</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reference / Resolution struct bytes</td><td>80 / 96</td></tr>
</table>
```

## Encrypted transport round-trip

`bench-iroh` times one full OCapN `greet` round-trip on loopback over
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh'))'s keyed QUIC and over cleartext TCP as a
baseline.

The first two charts are the **serial** case: one connection, one call in
flight. That is the worst case for the iroh path. The sidecar runs QUIC on its
own tokio runtime, and the synchronous driver blocks the calling thread on each
read, so every serial round-trip pays a cross-thread wake. Most of the gap to
TCP is that scheduling hop, not AEAD. Read serial latency as latency, not as the
transport's throughput ceiling.

The third chart shows why that ceiling is illusory: on one readiness loop
([ADR-0057](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0057-iroh-completion-loop-bridge.md))
per-connection round-trips overlap, so one thread sustains several times the
single-stream throughput. Both bars in that chart are iroh against itself.

None of this is field latency. Loopback removes propagation delay and packet
loss. Measure: `zig build bench-iroh` (needs `cargo`).

```=html
