## The same app on pancake and Goblins

One Racket application runs unchanged on pancake's Racket binding and on
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/), timed head to head
(`bench-goblins`: echo, capability registry, mint/purse, task board). Fairness
model:
[bench README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bench/goblins/README.md).

Read the pancake bars carefully: they are not pancake's Zig actor runtime. The
app's actors run inside the Racket binding's own small vat; pancake supplies the
wire, session, and codec underneath. So this pits protocol cores under one
Racket application style. It does not measure native Zig actors, production
networking, or many connections at once. Within those bounds the charts carry
the ordering.

The **pancake + time-travel** series turns snapshot on for every actor, so the
vat keeps revisable history. On light workloads it lands near plain pancake; on
heavier ones it costs real throughput plus the memory of that history. The
section below measures the snapshot operation itself. Measure:
`zig build bench-goblins` (needs `racket` + `goblins`; `-- --smoke` for a fast
pass).

```=html
